          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "Low iron can make people tired, but the material I searched does not explain the mechanism in detail.\n\nWhat the reference material does say: iron is needed to make hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. When iron stores run out, the red cells are made smaller and paler than normal. This is called iron-deficiency anemia (Source: \"Iron-deficiency anemia,\" General clinical reference). The link to tiredness is implied by that oxygen-carrying role, but the passage does not spell out the step from low hemoglobin to fatigue, so I can't confirm the details from these sources.\n\nThe same reference makes a point that matters for anyone worried about this: in an adult who is not menstruating heavily or pregnant, low iron is a finding that calls for an explanation, because iron is normally lost only through bleeding. A clinician would usually want to find out why the iron is low.\n\nOther sources I found, on thyroid problems and heart failure, also list tiredness among their symptoms, but they do not address iron and are not relevant here.\n\nIf you feel unusually tired, or have noticed other changes, a doctor can check your blood count and iron levels and talk through possible causes with you. If you have chest pain, severe breathlessness, fainting, or vomiting blood or black stools, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
